Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is \u201cfinancial point of view\u201d the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: ARTICLE 9. Evaluation of Proposals. <omitted> (b) The Company Board may, at any time prior to the closing of the transaction, make a recommendation change if it determines in good faith, after consultation with its financial advisors and legal counsel, that an Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. Such determination shall consider the financial aspects of the proposal, as well as strategic benefits, potential regulatory hurdles, and the impact on employees and stakeholders. The Company Board must also consider the long-term value creation potential of the proposal, beyond immediate financial gains. In making such a determination, the Company Board shall take into account the advice of a financial advisor of nationally recognized standing and legal counsel. (c) The Company shall provide written notice to Parent of the Company Board's intention to make a recommendation change, including detailed reasoning and any supporting documentation. Parent shall be given three Business Days to respond to the notice, during which the Company shall negotiate in good faith with Parent to amend the terms of the existing agreement to render the Acquisition Proposal no longer a Superior Proposal. If no agreement is reached, the Company Board may proceed with the recommendation change. (Pages 112-113)\nAnswer:
Choices:

