Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.4. No Solicitation. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, during the Pre-Closing Period, the Company Board may effect a Company Adverse Recommendation Change <omitted> if, and only if, <omitted> (ii) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, that the failure to make the Company Adverse Recommendation Change or terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (iii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board’s intention to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change or terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement at least three Business Days prior to such action, and (iv) the decision to make a Company Adverse Recommendation Change is in connection with an Acquisition Proposal or with the Company’s intent to terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement, and the Company shall have complied with the following: (1) prior to giving effect to clauses (2) through (4), the Company Board shall have determined that such Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, (2) the Company shall have made available to Parent the material terms and conditions of such Acquisition Proposal and copies of all relevant communications relating to such Acquisition Proposal, (3) the Company shall have negotiated in good faith with Parent during the three Business Day period provided in clause (iii) of this Section 9.4(c) with respect to such proposed revisions to this Agreement or other proposals made by Parent, if any, so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer constitute a Superior Proposal, (4) after considering the results of negotiations with Parent and taking into account the proposals made by Parent, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, the Company Board shall have determined in good faith that such Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal. (Pages 102-103)\nAnswer:
Choices:

